 \subsection{Where the Sudoku logic shows up}
 \label{sec:discussion-summary}
 
-The three confirmatory results give a consistent, if partly negative, picture of one coarse-to-fine Sudoku generator. Errors are most costly at the coarsest level: one replaced level-1 code lowered the solve share by \R{h2.est.abs} percentage points relative to one replaced level-11 code, while levels 2 and 11 were practically indistinguishable. Generating the later levels anew attenuated the damage of a level-1 replacement, by a small amount (\R{h3.est} points) whose practical relevance is not established. At the intermediate level~6, the candidate mask, a shallow rule that is exactly computable from the input, was readable with a linear probe only slightly above an unrelated state and below the registered practical threshold. At the last level the readout was good, but no better than from the teacher state that encodes the solution, and it was not equivariant under Sudoku symmetries.
+The three confirmatory results give a consistent, if partly negative, picture of one coarse-to-fine Sudoku generator. Errors are most costly at the coarsest level: one replaced level-1 code lowered the solve share by \R{h2.est.abs} percentage points relative to one replaced level-11 code, while levels 2 and 11 were practically indistinguishable. Generating the later levels anew attenuated the damage of a level-1 replacement, by a small amount (\R{h3.est} points) whose practical relevance is not established. At the intermediate level~6, the candidate mask, a shallow rule that is exactly computable from the input, was linearly readable above an unrelated state, but only by a small margin, below the registered practical threshold, and less well than from simple surface statistics of the puzzle. At the last level the readout was good, but no better than from the teacher state that encodes the solution; in an exploratory analysis its per-cell predictions were not equivariant under the sampled Sudoku symmetries at the fixed tolerance.
 
-Taken together, the results are consistent with a hierarchy in which a coarse level carries something the solution depends on, while the cell content becomes decodable only in the late levels (Section~\ref{sec:e1}); the candidate structure of the puzzle is not visible in linearly readable form part-way through. Each part of this picture is conditional on the checkpoint, the replacement policy, the probe family and its training budget. In particular, none of the results shows that the coarse code is a plan of a solving algorithm, that the model repairs logical errors, or that the intermediate state lacks candidate information that another readout could find.
+Taken together, the results are consistent with a hierarchy in which a coarse level carries something the solution depends on, while the final decoder recovers most of the cell content only from the late levels (Section~\ref{sec:e1}); part-way through, the candidate structure of the puzzle is linearly readable only weakly, below the practical threshold. Each part of this picture is conditional on the checkpoint, the replacement policy, the probe family and its training budget. In particular, none of the results shows that the coarse code is a plan of a solving algorithm, that the model repairs logical errors, or that the intermediate state lacks candidate information that another readout could find.
 
 \subsection{Relation to prior work}
 \label{sec:discussion-related}
 
-Linear readouts of Sudoku candidates, and a causally verified circuit for naked singles, have been reported for an autoregressive transformer trained on solver traces \citep{kniazev2026linear}; in that work unit-level probes reached perfect exact match while per-cell candidate probes did not. In a hierarchical reasoning model, probe directions for Sudoku were decodable but not more causal than random directions \citep{rodrigues2026hrm}. Our setting differs in the object of study: a non-autoregressive generator whose intermediate states are built from discrete, level-indexed codes. In this setting the candidate mask was barely linearly readable at level~6, and the unit-level target was not readable from the generated level-6 state, while both were readable from the input. Whether the difference comes from the architecture, the training objective or the readout cannot be decided from one checkpoint. Generated NCM codes have also been reported to be largely non-canonical under re-encoding \citep{khoury2026roundtrip}, which is one reason why we report cell-level outcomes next to every code-level count; the compensation we measure is related to, and kept distinct from, self-repair within a single forward pass \citep{ahmad2026dose}. The probing methodology follows the control-task tradition \citep{hewitt2019control,belinkov2022probing}.
