-The protocol, hypotheses, thresholds, sample sizes and interpretation rules were registered before TEST existed (OSF, \url{https://osf.io/m28fx}, embargoed until publication; repository tag \texttt{prereg-v3.7}). The primary family has three contrasts, H1 to H3, each tested with a two-sided Bonferroni interval of \R{level.family}\% nominal coverage, so that the family-wise error rate is at most \R{stat.alpha}\%. Intervals are percentile cluster bootstraps over puzzles with \R{stat.nboot} resamples; when fewer than twenty puzzles have a nonzero difference in a contrast, a conservative rare-event bound replaces the bootstrap. The practical thresholds were fixed in advance: \R{plan.h2practical} percentage points for H2, \R{plan.h3practical} for H3, an equivalence range of $\pm$\R{plan.theta2pmargin} points for the secondary comparison of levels 2 and 11, and $\pm$\R{plan.h1margin} in Brier loss for H1, whose equivalence reading uses a two one-sided test at the family level (\R{level.tost}\% interval). Each confirmatory result is reported with the claim it supports and the claims that do not follow from it, as fixed in the registered interpretation table.
+The protocol, hypotheses, thresholds, sample sizes and interpretation rules were registered before any model run on TEST and before any TEST outcome existed (OSF, \url{https://osf.io/m28fx}, embargoed until publication; repository tag \texttt{prereg-v3.7}). The primary family has three contrasts, H1 to H3, each tested with a two-sided Bonferroni interval of \R{level.family}\% nominal coverage, targeting a family-wise error rate of \R{stat.alpha}\%; bootstrap coverage is approximate. Intervals are percentile cluster bootstraps over puzzles with \R{stat.nboot} resamples; when fewer than twenty puzzles have a nonzero difference in a contrast, a conservative rare-event bound replaces the bootstrap. The practical thresholds were fixed in advance: \R{plan.h2practical} percentage points for H2, \R{plan.h3practical} for H3, an equivalence range of $\pm$\R{plan.theta2pmargin} points for the secondary comparison of levels 2 and 11, and $\pm$\R{plan.h1margin} in Brier loss for H1, whose equivalence reading uses a two one-sided test at the family level (\R{level.tost}\% interval). Each confirmatory result is reported with the claim it supports and the claims that do not follow from it, as fixed in the registered interpretation table.
 
-All analysis code was frozen under a git tag before any model ran on TEST. A dry run of the frozen code on non-TEST data had to reproduce earlier pilot outputs exactly before TEST could start, and a pre-written script evaluated TEST once. One defect found in the dry run was fixed, and the code re-tagged, before any TEST run, and one optional study was dropped; these and the remaining post-registration decisions are listed in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix}.
+\paragraph{Changes before the lock.} Three design choices were changed during preparation, on calibration and probe-selection data only, before the registration and before TEST was used: the primary H2 contrast moved from level~2 to level~1, after a pilot in which levels 2 to 11 barely changed the solve share; H3 moved to level~1 as well, because at level~2 decoding a replaced code never changed the solve outcome, so the compensation contrast there could only measure regeneration; and the primary H1 probe changed from a per-cell probe to the cell-relative whole-layer probe of Section~\ref{sec:e3-design}, with H1 recast as a two-sided contrast with an equivalence range, after the per-cell estimate on PROBE-VAL came out close to zero. The registration stated the expected H1 outcome as readability above zero but inside the equivalence range. All three changes were logged with their reasons. The H1 changes make an absence claim harder rather than easier to reach; the H2 and H3 changes, in contrast, moved the tests to the level where the pilot had shown an effect, so their confirmatory value rests on the fresh TEST puzzles.
+
+\paragraph{Execution.} All analysis code was frozen under a git tag before any model ran on TEST. A dry run of the frozen code on non-TEST data had to reproduce earlier pilot outputs exactly before TEST could start, and a pre-written script evaluated TEST once. Decisions taken after the registration are listed in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-deviations}.
